  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>You can absolutely personalize your rental without losing your deposit. The trick is to stick with changes that are removable, reversible, or that you can take with you, and to document the unit before you start. Here are eight renter-friendly upgrades that make a plain apartment feel like home and still come off clean at move-out.</p>

<h2>Start with your lease and move-in photos</h2>
<p>Before you buy a single thing, reread your lease. Some landlords ban all nail holes, others allow small picture-hanging holes, and a few let you paint if you repaint before you leave. Whatever the rules are, get any special permission in writing.</p>
<p>Then take date-stamped photos and video of every wall, floor, fixture and appliance. If your property gives you a move-in condition checklist, fill it out carefully and keep a copy. That record is what protects you if there is a dispute later, and it is the same habit you will use later on a <a href="/new-construction-walkthrough-punch-list/">new construction walkthrough punch list</a> when you build your own place.</p>

<h2>1. Use peel-and-stick for walls, backsplashes and floors</h2>
<p>Removable wallpaper, backsplash tiles and floor decals can change the whole feel of a kitchen, bathroom or accent wall in an afternoon. Quality matters here: better products tend to release cleanly, while bargain versions are more likely to leave residue or pull paint.</p>
<ul>
<li>Test a small piece in a closet or behind furniture for a couple of weeks.</li>
<li>Avoid applying to freshly painted walls; paint usually needs time to fully cure.</li>
<li>Keep leftover pieces so you can patch a damaged section instead of redoing everything.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2. Layer rugs to add warmth and hide tired floors</h2>
<p>Rugs are one of the best renter investments because they move with you. A large neutral base rug plus a smaller patterned one on top adds texture and color without overwhelming the room. Rugs also protect the floor from chair scuffs and furniture dents, which are common deposit deductions.</p>
<p>A washable, low-profile [[amz:area_rug|area rug]] is easy to clean and works on both carpet and hard floors. Add a non-slip pad so it stays put and does not scratch the finish underneath.</p>

<h2>3. Swap hardware and fixtures (and keep the originals)</h2>
<p>Cabinet knobs, drawer pulls and even shower heads are quick, inexpensive ways to give a kitchen or bath a custom look. Put every original screw and knob in a labeled zip bag and keep it somewhere you will not lose it. Before you move out, reinstall the originals and take your upgrades to the next place.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Tape the bag of original hardware inside the cabinet under the sink. You will always know where it is, and it reminds you to swap everything back during move-out cleaning.</p></div>

<h2>4. Hang art and shelves with removable strips</h2>
<p>Adhesive hooks and picture-hanging strips let you build a gallery wall, hang mirrors and even mount light shelves without a hammer. The key is matching the strip to the weight of the item and following the cure time on the package before hanging anything heavy.</p>
<p>A variety pack of [[amz:command_strips|removable picture-hanging strips]] covers most frames and small decor. When it is time to remove them, pull the tab straight down along the wall slowly rather than out toward you.</p>
<!--products:command_strips,area_rug,string_lights|Renter-friendly decor that comes off clean-->

<h2>5. Bring in plants (real or faux)</h2>
<p>Greenery makes any rental feel more alive. Pothos, snake plants, spider plants and succulents are forgiving and handle typical apartment light. Use saucers or plant trays under every pot, because water rings on windowsills and floors are an easy deduction to avoid. If your space is dim, a small clip-on grow light keeps herbs and houseplants healthy. Not a plant person? Good faux plants still add color with zero watering.</p>

<h2>6. Upgrade the lighting</h2>
<p>Builder-grade overhead lighting is usually harsh. Floor lamps, table lamps and plug-in wall sconces change the mood without touching the wiring. Swap in warm-white [[amz:led_bulbs|LED bulbs]] to make rooms feel cozier and trim your electric bill; just store the original bulbs to put back if your lease requires it. String lights along a headboard or window add a soft glow and come down in seconds.</p>

<h2>7. Dress the windows</h2>
<p>Curtains soften a room, add color and give you privacy. Tension rods and adhesive or no-drill curtain brackets mean you can hang them without new holes. In Texas heat, [[amz:blackout_curtains|blackout curtains]] also help keep sun-facing rooms cooler and can make bedrooms much better for sleep.</p>

<h2>8. Personalize with textiles and movable furniture</h2>
<p>The fastest personality boost is fabric: throw pillows, blankets, a new duvet, a fun shower curtain. These cost little, change with the seasons, and never touch the walls. Pair them with versatile pieces you will keep using, like a storage ottoman, a bookshelf or a rolling cart, so your money builds toward a future home instead of staying behind in the apartment.</p>
<!--products:blackout_curtains,storage_ottoman,shower_curtain|Easy upgrades you can take to your next place-->

<h2>Quick reference: safe vs. risky upgrades</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Usually safe</th><th>Ask first</th><th>Usually not allowed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Rugs, curtains on tension rods, lamps</td><td>Painting, small nail holes</td><td>Drilling into tile or cabinets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Removable strips and hooks</td><td>Peel-and-stick wallpaper</td><td>Changing locks yourself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swapping bulbs and knobs (keep originals)</td><td>Replacing light fixtures</td><td>Removing walls, fixtures or carpet</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Every lease is different, so treat this as a starting point and confirm with your property manager.</p>

<h2>Renting now, building later</h2>
<p>Many of our readers rent while they save for or plan a home build. Personalizing smartly now teaches you what you actually like, from lighting temperature to storage needs, which makes design decisions much easier later. Keep notes on what works. When you start thinking about <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">choosing a floor plan for your new home</a>, those notes become real requirements instead of guesses. If you are weighing your options, our guide on <a href="/how-to-turn-your-first-apartment-into-a-stepping-stone-for-home-investments/">turning your first apartment into a stepping stone</a> is a good read.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Pick two or three upgrades from this list, document your unit, and start small. When you are ready to think beyond the apartment, look at <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">budgeting for your Texas home build</a> and sign up for our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> to see what owner-building really involves.</p>
